      "Work through what the points actually do to the yield, because it is the part that makes short paper attractive and the part borrowers argue about. Two points on a six-month loan is four percent annualised on top of the rate — a twelve percent note with two points, held six months, is a sixteen percent annualised return. Hold the same note nine months and the points earn less annualised, which is the quiet reason lenders prefer short terms and extensions with fresh points over long original terms. It is also why a lender who is repaid early is usually pleased rather than disappointed: the points were earned in full and the capital is free to turn again.",
