      "Perfection is a technical subject that decides commercial outcomes, so it is worth understanding rather than delegating blindly. A security interest attaches when value is given and the debtor has rights in the collateral, and it is perfected — made good against other creditors — usually by filing a UCC-1 financing statement in the right office, which for most debtors is the state where the entity was organised rather than where the equipment sits. Get the debtor's exact legal name wrong, file in the wrong state, or describe the collateral too narrowly, and the filing can be ineffective against a competing creditor who got it right. The filing lapses after five years unless continued. None of this is difficult; all of it is fatal when skipped.",
      "Priority is the second half of the same subject. First to file or perfect generally wins, which means the question that matters before you lend is not whether you can take a security interest but whether anyone already has. A search of the debtor's filings will usually show a bank with a blanket lien over all assets, and a blanket lien filed three years ago beats your specific lien filed today. The answer is a written subordination or a release, obtained before funding. Purchase-money security interests in specific new equipment can take priority over an earlier blanket lien if the statutory steps are followed exactly and on time — which is a genuinely useful tool and an unforgiving one.",
      "Then the part that decides recoveries: what the collateral is actually worth to someone who is not the borrower. There are three values and lenders routinely confuse them. Book value is an accounting artefact. Fair market value assumes a willing buyer and an unhurried sale. Orderly liquidation value assumes a sale within a few months, and forced liquidation value assumes an auction next week — which is the only one that matters when you are seizing it. On general-purpose equipment the gap between fair market and forced liquidation might be forty percent. On a machine built to one customer's specification it can approach total. Underwrite to forced liquidation value on a third-party opinion, and treat everything above it as upside rather than as cushion.",
